XSW1..XSW8.xml are the output of this plugin's own applyXsw, run against the two
signed fixtures in tests/signing. They are committed so any change to the tree
surgery shows up as a byte diff rather than a silent behaviour change.

XSW1 and XSW2 wrap signed-response-by-us.xml (signature over the Response).
XSW3..XSW8 wrap signed-by-us.xml (signature over the Assertion).

Independent verdict, signxml 5.1.0 verifying the whole document as a service
provider would, 2026-08-06:

  control  signed-by-us.xml           VERIFIED  signed=Assertion ID=_a1
  control  signed-response-by-us.xml  VERIFIED  signed=Response  ID=_r1

  XSW1  REJECTED  DocumentInvalid    Response not expected inside Signature
  XSW2  VERIFIED  signed=Response  ID=_r1
  XSW3  VERIFIED  signed=Assertion ID=_a1
  XSW4  VERIFIED  signed=Assertion ID=_a1
  XSW5  VERIFIED  signed=Assertion ID=_a1
  XSW6  REJECTED  DocumentInvalid    Assertion not expected inside Signature
  XSW7  REJECTED  InvalidInput       Ambiguous reference URI #_a1 resolved to 2 nodes
  XSW8  REJECTED  InvalidInput       Ambiguous reference URI #_a1 resolved to 2 nodes

Reading that honestly:

XSW2, XSW3, XSW4 and XSW5 defeat a hardened independent verifier. It reports a
valid signature over the original element while a consumer that reads the first
or last Assertion gets the attacker's copy. Those are working attacks.

XSW1 and XSW6 are rejected only because signxml validates against the XML-DSig
schema, which allows just ds:Object inside a Signature. Service providers that do
not schema-validate remain vulnerable, so these variants stay in the tree.

XSW7 and XSW8 are rejected because signxml refuses to resolve a duplicate ID.
That is the secure behaviour, and it is what this plugin's own verifier reports
as ReferenceAmbiguous rather than guessing which element was signed.
